<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caodaism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1926</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Cao Đài</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Caodaism is a syncretic religion founded in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> in 1926, with its Holy See at Tây Ninh. It combines elements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, and honours figures such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a> as saints. In the late colonial period and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-vietnam">State of Vietnam</a> it raised its own armed forces, until Ngô Đình Diệm subdued them in 1955.</p></div>
